ndition \\(a\\ge n-k\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(a+b\\le k+2\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_{k+1}'\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{k+1}'|=\\frac{(k+1)(k+2)}2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n---\n\n**Step\u202fC \u2013 sunny lines of slope\u202f1.**  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + j .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines (6) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nInside the region (4) the line \\(L_j\\) consists of the points whose difference \\(b-a\\) equals \\(j\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nL_j\\cap T_{k+1}'=\n\\bigl\\{(1,1+j),\\,(2,2+j),\\dots ,\\bigl(k+1-j,\\;k+1\\bigr)\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{k+1}'\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step\u202fD \u2013 adjusting the number of sunny lines.**  \nThe line \\(L_0\\) is horizontal (\\(y=x\\) with intercept \\(0\\)), which is **not** sunny.  \nReplace \\(L_0\\) by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n-k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V\\) belongs to the set of non\u2011sunny lines and contains exactly the points of \\(T_{k+1}'\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(n-k\\); these are precisely the points that were on \\(L_0\\).  \n\nNow we have the following collection of lines:\n\n* the \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines (3);\n* the vertical line (8);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)+1+k=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)).  \nBy construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, completing the configuration.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The special case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) the bound (2) allows \\(k=3\\).  \nApplying the construction with \\(k=3\\) gives the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n