ce (4) is false. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f8 \u2013 Claim that the family \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T'_k\\)  \n\n> \u201cThe family \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T'_k\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSince the description of the intersection in (4) is incorrect, the assertion that the union of the \\(L_j\\) covers all of \\(T'_k\\) is unfounded. A simple counterexample: take \\(k=2\\); then \\(T'_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). The lines are \\(L_0: y=x\\) and \\(L_1: y=x+1\\). Their intersections with \\(T'_2\\) are \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,2)\\}\\) respectively; the point \\((2,1)\\) remains uncovered. Hence the claim is false \u2013 a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f9 \u2013 Replacement of \\(L_0\\) by a vertical line  \n\n> \u201cReplace the line \\(L_0\\) (which is horizontal when \\(j=0\\) \u2013 a non\u2011sunny line) by the vertical line  \n> \\[\n> V:\\;x=n-k .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n\\(L_0\\) is defined as \\(y=x\\), which has slope\u202f1, **not** slope\u202f0. Therefore it is a sunny line, not a horizontal (non\u2011sunny) line. The statement \u201c\\(L_0\\) is horizontal\u201d is factually incorrect, making this a **Critical Error**. Moreover, removing \\(L_0\\) eliminates a line that (even under the false premise) was supposed to cover part of the uncovered set, so the replacement does not repair the coverage gap identified in Step\u202f8.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f10 \u2013 Claim that the final set of lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\)  \n\n> \u201cThe set consisting of the lines (1), the line (5) and the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause Steps\u202f5\u20139 are incorrect, the coverage claim cannot hold. A concrete counterexample demonstrates the failure:\n\n- Take \\(n=5,\\ k=2\