            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is *non‑sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=0\\) is always possible  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.  \nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is admissible for every }n\\ge 3 } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The value \\(k=1\\) is always possible  \n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand one additional line \\(\\ell\\) defined by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=mx+b,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nchosen so that \\((n,1)\\in\\ell\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nThe only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\); by construction it belongs to \\(\\ell\\).  \n\nThe slope \\(m\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, while the \\(\\ell_i\\) are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently we have a covering consisting of exactly one sunny line:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=1\\text{ is admissible for every }n\\ge 3 } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the required set is  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAssume that three distinct lines \\(\\Lambda_1,\\Lambda_2,\\Lambda_3\\) cover \\(S_3\\) and that at least two of them are sunny.  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to the axes nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence its slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*If a sunny line contains two points of \\(S_3\\) whose coordinates differ by \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)\\), then its slope equals \\(\\Delta b/\\Delta a\\).  \nThe only possible slopes obtained from pairs of points of \\(S_3\\) are \\(0,\\infty,1,-1,2,-2,\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12\\).  \nAmong these, the admissible (sunny) slopes are \\(1,2,-2,\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12\\).*\n\nA direct inspection shows that any line with one of the admissible slopes can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_3\\) whose sum of coordinates equals \\(4\\) (the diagonal \\(a+b=4\\)).  \nConsequently, if two distinct sunny lines are used, they together cover at most two points of the diagonal \\(a+b=4\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(a+b=4\\) contains the three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); at least one of them would remain uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nHence a covering of \\(S_3\\) by three lines can contain **at most one** sunny line.  \nTogether with the constructions of Sections 1 and 2 we