            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and\n\\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Simple coverings giving \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n*Vertical covering.*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n*One‑sunny covering.*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(\\ell_i:x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) and the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=2x-(2n-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) are on the vertical lines; the only\nremaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  Hence \\(k=1\\) is always possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nReplace all sunny lines by non‑sunny vertical lines wherever possible:\nremove any sunny line that is not needed for covering and, if necessary,\nreplace it by a vertical line that still covers all points it previously\ncovered (this never increases the number of sunny lines).  \nConsequently we may assume that the configuration contains exactly\n\\(n-2\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and two sunny lines.\n\nThe \\(n-2\\) vertical lines can be taken as \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\); they cover all\npoints whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-2\\).  \nThe points that remain uncovered are\n\n\\[\nA=(n-1,1),\\qquad B=(n-1,2),\\qquad C=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line through two of \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) has slope\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{n-1-(n-1)}\\;(=0),\\qquad\n\\frac{2-1}{n-1-n}\\;(=\\infty),\\qquad\n\\frac{1-2}{n-(n-1)}\\;(=-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); all three are non‑sunny.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points, so at least\nthree sunny lines are required – a contradiction.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=2\\text{ is impossible for every }n\\ge3.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A core of three sunny lines covering the last three columns\n\nConsider the six points\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1&=(n-2,1), & P_2&=(n-1,2),\\\\\nP_3&=(n-2,2), & P_4&=(n,1),\\\\\nP_5&=(n-2,3), & P_6&=(n-1,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nDefine the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_A&:\\;y-x=-(n-3) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n\\ell_B&:\\;2y+x=2n-3 &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n\\ell_C&:\\;y+2x=2n-5 &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\nP_1 & \\ell_A\\\\\nP_2 & \\ell_A\\\\\nP_3 & \\ell_B\\\\\nP_4 & \\ell_B\\\\\nP_5 & \\ell_C\\\\\nP_6 & \\ell_C\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the three lines cover exactly the six points of the last three columns.\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\);\nhence they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Extending the construction to any \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nFor each column \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(i,n+1-i)\n\\]\n\n(the highest point of that column).  \nJoin \\(Q_i\\) with the fixed point \\(P_1=(n-2,1)\\); the resulting line is\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{\\,n-2-i\\,}\\,(x-i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope in (1) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i-n}{\\,n-2-i\\,}\\).\nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{i-n}{\\,n-2-i\\,}\\neq0,\\qquad\n\\frac{i-n}{\\,n-2-i\\,}\\neq\\infty,\\qquad\n\\frac{i-n}{\\,n-2-i\\,}\\neq-1,\n\\]\n\nso each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  Different values of \\(i\\) give different slopes,\nhence the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\nNow fix a number \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k-3\\) and set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{\\ell_A,\\ell_B,\\ell_C\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,x=j\\mid j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\setminus I\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains\n\n* the three core sunny lines,\n* exactly \\(k-3\\) additional sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\),\n* the remaining \\(n-3-(k-3)=n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n– Every point in columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-3\\) lies either on the vertical line\n\\(x=j\\) (if \\(j\\notin I\\)) or on the sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) (if \\(j\\in I\\)).  \n– All points of columns \\(n-2,n-1,n\\) are covered by the three core lines\n\\(\\ell_A,\\ell_B,\\ell_C\\) as shown in Section 3.  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\nSummarising the four parts:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – vertical covering (Section 1).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – one sunny line plus verticals (Section 1).  \n* \\(k=2\\) – impossible (Section 2).  \n* \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) – construction of Section 4.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
