(\\text{slope }-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution shows that they contain respectively the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line}\\\\ \\hline\n   (n-2,1) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n   (n-2,2) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n   (n,1)   & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & \\ell_{3}\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & \\ell_{3}\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the three lines cover **all** points whose first coordinate is \\(n-2,n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \n   The remaining points (those with first coordinate \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\)) are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Altogether we have \\( (n-3)+3 = n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of them sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n5. **Increasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the configuration of step\u202f4 (which has \\(k=3\\)), we can raise the number of sunny lines one by one until we reach \\(k=n\\).  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(3\\le k < n\\)) is already built.  \n   Because \\(n-k\\ge 1\\), at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines is vertical; say it is \\(x=c\\).  \n   The column \\(x=c\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (since \\(c\\le n-2\\)).  \n   Choose one of those points, call it \\(P\\).  \n   Replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by a **new sunny line** \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\(P\\) and has an irrational slope (for instance slope \\(\\sqrt2\\)).  \n   Such a line meets no other lattice point of \\(T_n\\); therefore all points that were covered before remain covered, and the new line adds exactly the point \\(P\\).  \n   The total number of lines stays \\(n\\), while the number of sunny lines increases by one (the former vertical line becomes sunny).  \n   Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with \\(k=4,5,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) because the initial configuration of step\u202f4 supplies a starting point for the induction.\n\nCombining steps\u202f2\u20135 we have proved that the only forbidden value of \\(k\\) is \\(2\\); all other integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) are attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y= \\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.\nReplace every non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) by a vertical line that passes through at least the same points of \\(T_n\\); this can only increase the set of covered points, so the new family still covers \\(T_n\\) and still has exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently we may assume that \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\n\\]\n\nand two sunny lines.\n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(n-1,1),\\qquad B=(n-1,2),\\qquad C=(n,1)\n\\]\n\nare not on any of the vertical lines.  \nThe slopes of the three possible lines through two of these points are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,B)=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(A,C)=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,C)=-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden, therefore **no sunny line can contain two of \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\)**; each sunny line can contain at most one of them.  \nWith only two sunny lines, at least one of \\(A,B,C\\) stays uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq 2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are all non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Choose any sunny line through this point, for example  \n\n  \\[\n  y=2x-(2n-1)\n  \\]\n\n  (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A base configuration with three sunny lines\n\nDefine the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&:\\;y = x-(n-3),\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&:\\;x+2y = n+2,\\\\\n\\ell_{3}&:\\;2x+y = 2n-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three have slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence they are sunny.\n\nA direct check shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point of }T_n & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(n-2,1) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n(n-1,2) & \\ell_{1}\\\\\n(n-2,2) & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n(n,1)   & \\ell_{2}\\\\\n(n-2,3) & \\ell_{3}\\\\\n(n-1,1) & \\ell_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the three lines cover every point whose first coordinate equals \\(n-2\\), \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).\n\nThe remaining points are those with first coordinate \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\); they are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nAltogether we have \\((n-3)+3=n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of them sunny, and every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line.  \nHence for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3\\text{ is attainable}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Raising the number of sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case.*  \\(k=3\\) is given by the construction of Section\u202f3.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(3\\le k<n\\)) exists.  \nBecause \\(n-k\\ge1\\), at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is vertical; denote it by \\(x=c\\).  \nSince \\(c\\le n-2\\), the column \\(x=c\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\); pick one of them and call it \\(P=(c,b)\\).\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) that is **irrational** (e.g. \\(\\alpha=\\sqrt2\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y-\\!b = \\alpha\\,(x-c) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, \\(\\ell\\) contains **no other lattice point** of the integer lattice, in particular no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) is sunny (its slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\)) and it covers exactly the point \\(P\\) among the points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nAll points that were covered before remain covered (the only point possibly lost is \\(P\\), which is now covered by \\(\\ell\\)).  \nThe total number of lines stays \\(n\\), while the number of sunny lines increases from \\(k\\) to \\(k+1\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy induction we obtain configurations for every \\(k=3,4,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Section\u202f1);\n* \\(k=0,1\\) are possible (Section\u202f2);\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) is possible (Sections\u202f3\u20134).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is solved.**"

