\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\,s_n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s_n=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n-2, & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(k=n\\) is never allowed, and when \\(n\\) is odd the value \\(k=n-1\\) is also forbidden.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(m=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use.  \n   We employ the following three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,m\\);\n   * horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,m\\);\n   * diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+2-m,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\n   Exactly \\(m\\) of these lines are taken (any choice of \\(m\\) distinct lines among the three families).  \n   Because each of the three families covers a whole \u201cside\u2019\u2019 of the triangle, the union of the chosen \\(m\\) lines contains **all** points whose first coordinate is \\(\\le m\\) **or** whose second coordinate is \\(\\le m\\) **or** whose sum of coordinates is \\(\\ge n+2-m\\).\n\n   The points that may still be uncovered are those satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge m+1,\\qquad b\\ge m+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Condition (5) describes a smaller right\u2011angled triangle\n   \\[\n   T_{k}^{\\;*}=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n   where we set \\(a'=a-m,\\;b'=b-m\\).  Hence the uncovered set is exactly a copy of \\(T_{k}^{\\;*}\\).\n\n   It remains to cover \\(T_{k}^{\\;*}\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because of (4) we have \\(k\\le s_n\\) when \\(n\\) is even and \\(k\\le s_n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd; in both cases \\(k\\le s_k:=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  \n   The following elementary pairing shows that \\(T_{k}^{\\;*}\\) can always be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2r\\).  \n   Partition the points of \\(