   \\]\n\n   because \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le t+1\\) imply \\(1\\le i\\le t\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n3. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (3).  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 reduction to a smaller triangle.*  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   obtained from the original points by the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m,b)\\).\n   Thus the uncovered set is a copy of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the set \\(T_k'\\) can be covered by the \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) of slope \\(1\\) given in (4) with \\(t=k\\).\n   These lines are sunny because their slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 total family.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(m\\) vertical lines (5) together with the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one\n   of them (by Steps\u202f1 and\u202f3).  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) that\n   satisfies (3).\n\n4. **Impossibility when \\(n\\) is even and \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each could contain at most \\(s_n=n/2\\) points\n   (by (1) with \\(n\\) even).  Consequently the total number of points that could\n   be covered would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac n2=\\frac{n^2}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is imposs