            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\nA sunny line has equation \\(y=-\\dfrac{p}{q}x+c\\) with coprime positive integers\n\\(p,q\\) and \\(p\\neq q\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \nAll integer points on the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0-tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is one lattice point on the line.\nBecause \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{n-x_0}{q},\\qquad\nt\\le\\frac{y_0-1}{p}.\n\\]\n\nHence the number of admissible integer points on the line does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{p,q\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\neq q\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), we have \\(\\max\\{p,q\\}\\ge2\\); inserting this\ninto (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{points of }T_n\\text{ on a sunny line}\\}\\le\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ns_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a non‑sunny line  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains the points\n\\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n\\) points.  \n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is analogous.  \n*Line of slope \\(-1\\)* has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) points.  \nConsequently any non‑sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.\nUsing the bounds from Sections 1 and 2,\n\n\\[\nk\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (4) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even; otherwise (5) imposes no\nfurther restriction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Covering a triangular set by sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 2.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by\nexactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe proceed by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=-2x+3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for \\(m-1\\).  \nConsider the point \\(P=(1,m)\\in T_m\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\;y=-2x+(2+m)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-2\\) (hence sunny) and passes through \\(P\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_m\\) that belong to \\(\\ell_m\\) are removed; the set that\nremains is a subset of \\(T_{m-1}\\) (every remaining point satisfies either\n\\(a\\ge2\\) or \\(b\\le m-1\\)).  \nBy the induction hypothesis those remaining points can be covered by\n\\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines, none of which coincides with \\(\\ell_m\\) because\n\\(\\ell_m\\) is the unique line of slope \\(-2\\) through \\((1,m)\\).  \nHence \\(T_m\\) is covered by the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (5) and set \\(m:=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\) lies on one of these\nlines.\n\n*Remaining points.*  \nAfter removing the points covered in (7) the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nBy Lemma 2, \\(T_k'\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines;\nfor instance we may use the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) defined in (6)\nwith the parameter \\(m\\) replaced by the appropriate index.  All these lines\nhave slope \\(-2\\) and are therefore sunny.\n\n*Total family.*  \nThe family consisting of the \\(m\\) vertical lines (7) together with the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines from Lemma 2 contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least\none of them (by construction).  Hence a configuration exists for every\n\\(k\\) obeying (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each could contain at most \\(s_n=\n\\frac{n}{2}\\) points of \\(T_n\\) by (3).  Consequently the total number of\ncovered points would be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_n|,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) cannot occur when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the construction (which works for every \\(k\\) satisfying (5)) with\nthe impossibility proved for even \\(n\\) and \\(k=n\\), we obtain exactly the\nset of admissible values:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\qquad \nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even.}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
