ger points on it are \\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0-tp)\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \n   Because \\(\\max\\{p,q\\}\\ge2\\), the \\(x\\)-coordinate changes by at least 2\n   when moving from one lattice point of the line to the next; consequently\n   at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.  \n   Since \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\),\n   we obtain the simpler bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{any sunny line meets at most } s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\text{ points of }T_n.}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Upper bound for a non‑sunny line.**  \n   A non‑sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\)\n   (\\(x+y=d\\)).  In each case the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on the line is at most \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Using the bounds above,\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) and solving (2) gives\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence any admissible configuration must satisfy (3).\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \n   Let \\(m:=n-k\\).  Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining set.*  \n   After removing the points covered in (4) the uncovered points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly a translate of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines covering \\(T'_k\\).*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y = -2x + (j+2) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n   Direct substitution shows that \\(\\ell_j\\) contains precisely the points of\n   \\(T'_k\\) with \\(a'=j\\) (i.e. the points \\((j,\\,k+1-j)\\) and, when \\(j\\le k\\),\n   also \\((j,\\,k+2-j)\\) if the latter lies inside the triangle).  In any case,\n   the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers every point of \\(T'_k\\); a short\n   case‑by‑case verification for the three possible values of \\(k\\bmod 2\\) is\n   given below.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Write a point of \\(T'_k\\) as \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\).  Set\n   \\(j:=a'\\).  Then \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) and \\(b'\\le k+1-j\\).  Substituting \\((a',b')\\) into\n   the equation of \\(\\ell_j\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b' = -2a' + (j+2)= -2j + (j+2)= -j+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\ell_j\\) contains exactly the point \\((j,\\,k+1-j)\\); if \\(k\\) is odd,\n   the middle point \\(\\bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2},\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr)\\) lies on\n   \\(\\ell_{(k+1)/2}\\) as well.  Consequently every point of \\(T'_k\\) appears on\n   one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – total family.*  \n   The \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines (4) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) give\n   exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and they cover\n   all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Thus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary inequality (3) with the explicit construction of\n   Step 5, we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny\n   lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n   \\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n\n   In pa