ent does not address them.  \n* Moreover, the condition \u201c\\(p\\neq q\\)\u201d only excludes slope \\(-1\\); it does not exclude horizontal (\\(p=0\\)) or vertical (\\(q=0\\)) lines, which are already excluded by the definition of \u201csunny\u201d. The passage does not justify why the chosen parametrisation captures *all* sunny lines. Hence the step lacks a rigorous justification.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Upper bound on the number of lattice points of a sunny line inside \\(T_n\\)\n\n> \u201cBecause \\(\\max\\{p,q\\}\\ge2\\), the \\(x\\)-coordinate changes by at least\u202f2 when moving from one lattice point of the line to the next; consequently at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \n* The argument correctly notes that for a line with rational slope \\(-p/q\\) (in lowest terms) the integer points are spaced by \\((q,-p)\\). Since \\(p\\neq q\\) and both are positive, indeed \\(\\max\\{p,q\\}\\ge2\\).  \n* However, the case \\(q=1\\) (so the \\(x\\)-step is 1) is not handled. When \\(q=1\\) the \\(x\\)-coordinate changes by 1, but the \\(y\\)-coordinate changes by at least 2, which may still allow more than \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor+1\\) points in the triangular region. A rigorous proof must consider both the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-directions and the shape of \\(T_n\\).  \n* The bound is asserted without a detailed counting argument; therefore the step contains a gap in justification.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Upper bound for a non\u2011sunny line  \n\n> \u201cA non\u2011sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=d\\)).  In each case the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on the line is at most \\(n\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct. For a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\le n\\); similarly for horizontal lines and lines of slope \\(-1\\) the number of integer solutions to \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) is \\(d-1\\le n\\). No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\n> \u201cSuppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Using the bounds above,\n> \\[\n> k\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n> =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** The inequality correctly expresses that the total *capacity* (maximum possible number of points each line can cover) must be at least the total number of points. The notation \\(s_n\\) is the bound from step\u202f3. No error here.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Solving the inequality  \n\n> \u201cSubstituting \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) and solving (2) gives\n> \\[\n> k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* Performing the algebra correctly yields  \n  \\[\n  k\\bigl(s_n-n\\bigr)\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2,\n  \\]  \n  which after dividing by the negative number \\(s_n-n\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-s_n)}.\n  \\]  \n  Since \\(n-s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), the resulting bound is  \n  \\[\n  k\\le n-1\\quad(\\text{if }n\\text{ even}),\\qquad k\\le n\\quad(\\text{if }n\\text{ odd}),\n  \\]  \n  i.e. a far weaker restriction than \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \n* The solution\u2019s claim that (2) leads to (3) is therefore algebraically false. This error breaks the logical chain that would give a necessary bound on \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7. Construction \u2013 vertical lines  \n\n> \u201cLet \\(m:=n-k\\).  Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct. These lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Description of the uncovered set  \n\n> \u201cAfter removing the points covered in (4) the uncovered points form the set  \n> \\[\n> T'_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\